          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I keep getting urine infections, three this year. Is recurrent infection something to escalate, and what does guidance describe about managing it?",
          "answer": "Three urine infections in one year is a pattern that guidance treats as \"recurrent.\" It is worth raising with a clinician, though this is not an emergency based on what you have told me.\n\n**What counts as recurrent.** The urinary tract infection guideline summary (Infectious diseases and urology society guidance) describes recurrence as two or more infections in six months, or three or more in a year. You meet the second definition.\n\n**What the guidance says about managing it.** The retrieved passages do not describe the prevention options in detail. The guideline summary says recurrence prevention is covered there, but that section did not come back in my search, so I cannot tell you what it recommends. The general reference (General clinical reference, urinary tract infection) says that urine culture is used when an infection is complicated or recurrent, which suggests a clinician may want a urine test to guide treatment.\n\n**Other points from the guidance:**\n- Treatment choice depends on local resistance patterns, so the right approach can vary by area.\n- If symptoms do not improve within about 48 hours, the guidance says to reassess and test, not to simply extend the same course.\n- Not every bacteria in urine needs treating. The guideline says that bacteria without symptoms usually should not be treated, except in pregnancy or before certain procedures.\n\n**Seek urgent care now if you have** fever with back or side pain, shaking chills, vomiting that stops you drinking, confusion, or symptoms that get worse despite treatment. Fever with a fast heartbeat, low blood pressure, or confusion together can signal sepsis, which is an emergency (Sepsis guidance).\n\n**Suggested next step:** book an appointment and tell the clinician about all three infections, when they happened, and whether each was confirmed by a urine test. They can explain prevention options and whether any underlying factors should be checked.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
